{
  "_meta": {
    "purpose": "Cross-library interop for the crypto-core 5 suites, generated ONCE by hand and never by a script in the repo: noble signs and encapsulates here, OpenSSL (pyca/cryptography + backend/tests/catkdf_ref.py) checks both and adds its own half, and noble checks those — crypto-core's test/hybrid.test.js on one side, backend/tests/test_hybrid_primitives.py on the other. Keys are hex (identifiers), signatures, ciphertexts and keys-for-AES base64 (payloads), as on the wire.",
    "noble": "@noble/post-quantum 0.7.1, @noble/curves 2.4.0",
    "openssl": "pyca/cryptography 50.0.2 (OpenSSL 4.0.3 29 Sep 2026)",
    "patterned_keys": "p384_composite_secret_key is the ML-DSA-65 seed d0..ef followed by the P-384 scalar 10..3f; catkdf_secret_key the P-384 scalar 60..8f followed by the ML-KEM-768 seed 90..cf. Test keys, on purpose, not credentials.",
    "p384_composite": "MLDSA65-ECDSA-P384-SHA512 (draft-ietf-lamps-pq-composite-sigs-19). Both signatures are over `message` and low-S — OpenSSL's normalized by hand, since it does not do so itself and the verifiers on both sides require it.",
    "catkdf": "ETSI TS 103 744 V1.2.2 CatKDF, HKDFwSHA384_P384_ML-KEM-768, laid out as crypto-core's kem.js does: public key P-384 point (SEC1 uncompressed) || ML-KEM-768 key, ciphertext ephemeral point || ML-KEM ciphertext; *_catkdf_key is the 32-byte AES key each ciphertext carries.",
    "p384_composite_address": "The address crypto-core 5.0.0 gives p384_composite_public_key: SHA-384 of 'Kryptolog address v5\\nalg=MLDSA65-ECDSA-P384-SHA512\\n' || key, hex. Computed by hand with Python's hashlib; crypto-core's addressOf and backend security.crypto_validation.address_of must both reproduce it.",
    "history": "The crypto-core 4 fixture (composite ML-DSA-65 + Ed25519, X-Wing, the Ed25519 torsion edges) was removed with those suites in the 5.0.0 cutover; git history keeps it."
  },
